.TH GAVEL 1 "2026-09-06" "gavel 0.2.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
gavel \- human review workstation for AI-flagged CERT-C findings
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gavel
[\fB\-\-db\fR \fIPATH\fR]
[\fB\-\-json\fR]
.I COMMAND
[\fIARGS\fR...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B gavel
is a terminal review workstation for adjudicating secure-coding findings
that an AI agent (Claude) flagged against CERT-C rules in C/C++ source.
An agent produces a batch of review items as JSON; a human imports them,
works through each one in a ratatui terminal UI, and exports adjudicated
verdicts back out as JSON for the agent to consume.
.PP
Every command supports
.B \-\-json
for machine-readable output (where applicable) and
.B \-\-db
.I PATH
to override database resolution.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B init
Initialize a new database at
.I .gavel/gavel.db
in the target directory (cwd by default, or
.B \-\-dir
.IR PATH ).
.TP
.B import \fIFILE\fR \fR[\fB\-\-decisions\fR \fILIST\fR]
Import review items from a JSON array or JSONL file (one object per line).
Each object has the shape:
.IR "{external_id?, title, rule_id, rule_text?, language, file_path?, start_line, code, context?}" .
.I external_id
is an optional caller-supplied id, opaque to gavel, echoed back verbatim
on
.B export
so a caller can join an exported verdict back to its own row (e.g. a row
in the caller's own tracking table) without depending on import order
surviving a review session. It is not used for
.B show / review \-\-id
resolution \(em that's what the internal uuid (or its prefix) is for.
Every imported item starts with status
.IR pending .
.B \-\-decisions
restricts the decisions
.B review
offers to a comma-separated subset of the five-value vocabulary (e.g.
.IR violation,false_positive,uncertain ),
in the order given \(em
.B review\(cq\&s
numbered decision keys are assigned
.I 1..N
in that order, so a value not in the list is never offered as a button.
Persists for the whole database until a later import passes a different
list; omit to leave (or keep) the full vocabulary offered. Prints the
count imported.
.TP
.B list \fR[\fB\-\-status\fR \fISTATUS\fR]
List review items as a table: short id, rule id, title, status.
.B \-\-status
filters to
.IR pending | in_review | adjudicated .
.TP
.B show \fIID\fR
Print full detail for one item: the code snippet with real line numbers,
rule text, context, any line comments, and the verdict if adjudicated.
Also prints
.I external_id
if the item was imported with one.
.I ID
accepts a full uuid or an unambiguous prefix (the short id shown by
.BR list ).
Non-interactive.
.TP
.B review \fR[\fB\-\-id\fR \fIID\fR] [\fB\-\-reviewer\fR \fINAME\fR]
Launch the interactive TUI. With no
.BR \-\-id ,
works through every
.I pending
/
.I in_review
item as a queue, in import order. With
.BR \-\-id ,
opens that one item directly regardless of its status.
.B \-\-reviewer
records a name on saved verdicts (defaults to
.BR $USER ).
See
.B KEYBINDINGS
below.
.TP
.B export \fR[\fB\-\-status\fR \fISTATUS\fR] [\fB\-o\fR \fIFILE\fR]
Export items as a JSON array, each carrying its
.I external_id
(or
.I null
if none was given on import), its verdict (or
.I null
if none), and its line comments.
.B \-\-status
filters to
.IR pending | in_review | adjudicated | all
(default
.IR adjudicated ,
since that's what an agent typically wants back).
.B \-o
writes to a file instead of stdout.
.TP
.B stats
Print counts of items by status and of verdicts by decision.
.SH KEYBINDINGS (review TUI)
.TP
.B j / k / \(dnarrow / \(uparrow
Move the selected line up/down in the code pane.
.TP
.B c
Add a comment on the currently selected line. Type the comment text,
.B Enter
to save,
.B Esc
to cancel.
.TP
.B 1..N
Set verdict, one key per decision currently offered. With the full
vocabulary (the default, no
.B \-\-decisions
given on import):
.B 1
compliant,
.B 2
violation,
.B 3
false_positive,
.B 4
needs_more_context,
.B 5
uncertain. If the importing
.B gavel import \-\-decisions
call narrowed the vocabulary, only that subset is offered, keyed
.I 1..N
in the order given at import \(em see
.B VERDICT DECISIONS
below.
.PP
After pressing a verdict key, type an optional rationale/notes,
.B Enter
to save the verdict and automatically advance to the next item in the
queue (or exit the TUI if it was the last one),
.B Esc
to cancel and stay on the current item.
.TP
.B n
Skip to the next item without recording a verdict.
.TP
.B p
Go back to the previous item.
.TP
.B PageUp / PageDown
Scroll the rule/context pane.
.TP
.B q
Quit. All comments and verdicts are already persisted to the database as
they're entered, so there is nothing to "save" on quit — items left
without a verdict simply remain
.I in_review
for the next
.B gavel review
session.
.SH ITEM STATUSES
.TP
.B pending
Freshly imported, not yet opened for review.
.TP
.B in_review
Opened in the TUI at least once but not yet adjudicated.
.TP
.B adjudicated
A verdict has been recorded.
.SH VERDICT DECISIONS
.B compliant, violation, false_positive, needs_more_context, uncertain.
.B review
offers all five unless a
.B gavel import \-\-decisions \fILIST\fR
call narrowed the set for this database (persists until a later import
passes a different list) \(em see
.B import
above.
.SH DATABASE RESOLUTION
.IP 1. 4
The
.B \-\-db
.I PATH
flag.
.IP 2. 4
The
.B GAVEL_DB
environment variable.
.IP 3. 4
A walk up from the current directory looking for a
.I .gavel/gavel.db
file (like git finds
.IR .git ).
.IP 4. 4
Otherwise the command exits with status 1 and a hint pointing at
.BR "gavel init" .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B GAVEL_DB
If set, used as the database path when no
.B \-\-db
flag is supplied.
.SH EXAMPLES
Initialize and import a batch of findings, then review them:
.RS
.nf
gavel init
gavel import findings.jsonl
gavel review
.fi
.RE
.PP
Check progress and export what's been decided so far:
.RS
.nf
gavel stats
gavel export --status adjudicated -o verdicts.json
.fi
.RE
.PP
Jump straight to one item by its short id (as shown by
.BR list ):
.RS
.nf
gavel show a1b2c3d4
gavel review --id a1b2c3d4
.fi
.RE
.SH EXIT CODES
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
User error: bad input, missing database, item not found.
.TP
.B 2
System error: unwritable path, corrupt database.
